Q: How does reading data with &gt;&gt; work inside a while loop in C++? A: <p>Have you read about <em>operator overloading</em> yet? Essentially there's a function like</p>
<pre><code>std::istream&amp; operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream&amp; stream, float&amp; value);
</code></pre>
<p>(for reading <code>float</code> values).</p>
<p>It attempts to read a <code>float</code> value from the stream, and then return the stream reference itself.</p>
<p>When you use multiple <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operators in a row, it's like chaining function calls. The expression <code>File &gt;&gt; l &gt;&gt; x &gt;&gt; w &gt;&gt; x &gt;&gt; h</code> can be seen as the separate operations</p>
<pre><code>std::istream&amp; temp1 = operator&gt;&gt;(File, l);
std::istream&amp; temp2 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp1, x);
std::istream&amp; temp3 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp2, w);
std::istream&amp; temp4 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp3, x);
std::istream&amp; temp5 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp4, h);
// temp1, temp2, temp2, temp4 and temp5 all references
// the original File stream object
</code></pre>
<p>Then <code>std::istream</code> has an overloaded conversion to <code>bool</code> operator, that can be used to check if an operation succeeded or failed.</p>
<p>So essentially the loop can be rewritten as</p>
<pre><code>while (File)
{
    std::istream&amp; temp1 = operator&gt;&gt;(File, l);
    if (temp1)
    {
        std::istream&amp; temp2 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp1, x);
        if (temp2)
        {
            std::istream&amp; temp3 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp2, w);
            if (temp3)
            {
                std::istream&amp; temp4 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp3, x);
                if (temp4)
                {
                    std::istream&amp; temp5 = operator&gt;&gt;(temp4, h);
                    if (temp5)
                    {
                        std::cout &lt;&lt; l &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; h;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
